# Printing: the spooler, the PostScript path, and what a
# printer driver actually receives.

shot    gs-devices
for     gs403 gs33
cap     Ghostscript's device list -- what this build can rasterise to.  Both
cap     Ghostscripts are here; only 4.03 has its initialisation files.
run     export GS_LIB=/dd/LIB/gs403
wait    2
run     gs403 -h | tail -l=16
wait    12

shot    gs403
cap     Aladdin Ghostscript 4.03 on an OS-9 disk: its banner, its switches
cap     and the devices it can write.  It does NOT render here -- asked to
cap     rasterise one of its own test files to a PBM, with GS_LIB pointing
cap     at LIB/gs403, it prints the banner and stops without writing a file
cap     or saying why.  Measured 2026-08-28.
run     gs403 -h
wait    10

shot    lp
size    36 80
for     lp lpq lpsched splstat
cap     The print spooler with nothing configured, which is the state a
cap     fresh disk arrives in: `lpq' says there is no spooler installed and
cap     `lp' says what it would take.  Carl Kreider's `splstat' prints
cap     nothing whatever, so its line here is just the command.
cap     Until 2026-08-29 there were TWO cards for this, `lp' and `lpq',
cap     crediting almost the same eight programs between them and running
cap     three; the five that were never typed are on the card below.
run     lp -?
wait    6
run     lpq
wait    5
run     lpsched -?
wait    6
run     splstat
wait    5

shot    spooler-rest
size    36 80
for     lprm lpshut submit prjob qp
cap     The rest of the spooler set, each asked for itself.  `lprm' removes
cap     a queued job, `lpshut' stops the scheduler, `submit' and `prjob'
cap     queue work, and `qp' is the queue printer -- which is one of the
cap     seven programs that fail for want of a program called `shell'
cap     (DOC/STATUS names them all).  With no spooler installed none of
cap     them has anything to act on, and what each says about that is the
cap     point of the card.
run     lprm -?
wait    6
run     lpshut -?
wait    6
run     submit -?
wait    6
run     prjob -?
wait    6
run     qp
wait    8

shot    lwf
for     lwf
cap     lwf turns plain text into PostScript, the way enscript does.  Its
cap     prologue comes from USR/LIB; here is the head of a page it set.
run     lwf /dd/SYS/motd | head -n 18
wait    10

shot    perr
cap     perr turns an OS-9 error number into its message.  216 is the one
cap     this collection meets most.
run     perr 214 216 218 221
wait    4

shot    perr-alps
for     perr alps epson
cap     perr names an OS-9 error; alps switches an ALPS printer between
cap     draft and letter quality; epson drives an Epson from spline output.
run     perr 1 2 3 4 5
wait    6
run     alps -?
wait    6

shot    perr-print
for     perr
cap     perr turns an OS-9 error number into its message -- the errors this
cap     collection meets most.
run     perr 214 216 218 221 237
wait    4

shot    spooler
for     splman splprt splstat gs33
cap     Ghostscript 3.33, the older of the two -- it lists its devices and
cap     then says what it is missing, which is its initialisation files.
cap     Carl Kreider's spooler (splman, splprt, splstat) is named here too
cap     because all three print NOTHING at all, with or without a queue.
run     gs33 -h | head -n 14
wait    12